        q: "5. Art vs commercial cinema",
        a: "Art cinema and commercial cinema represent two different approaches to filmmaking. \nWhile art cinema focuses more on artistic expression and meaningful themes, commercial \ncinema mainly focuses on entertainment and mass appeal. \n\n \n1. Purpose: Art cinema focuses on artistic expression, realism and meaningful storytelling, \nwhereas commercial cinema aims to entertain a large audience and achieve box-office \nsuccess. \n2. Themes: Art films often deal with social issues, human relationships, politics and \neveryday life. Commercial films commonly use themes such as romance, action, \ncomedy, family drama and adventure. \n3. Storytelling: Art cinema often uses realistic, experimental and unconventional storytelling \ntechniques. Commercial cinema generally follows more familiar and accessible narrative \npatterns. \n4. Audience: Art cinema usually attracts a smaller and specialised audience, while \ncommercial cinema is designed for a mass audience. \n5. Examples: Filmmakers such as Satyajit Ray, Mrinal Sen and Shyam Benegal are \nassociated with art cinema, while mainstream filmmakers create commercial films with \npopular stars, music and entertainment. \nBoth art and commercial cinema have contributed to the development of Indian cinema. While \nart cinema encourages creativity and social awareness, commercial cinema makes films \naccessible to a wider audience through entertainment."
