        q: "12. Rashomon - Japanese cinema",
        a: "Rashomon is a landmark Japanese film directed by Akira Kurosawa and released in 1950. \nIt had a major impact on Japanese cinema and introduced Japanese filmmaking to international \naudiences. \n1. Multiple perspectives: The film presents the same incident from different characters’ \nviewpoints, showing that people may have different versions of the same truth. \n2. Innovative storytelling: Kurosawa used a non-linear narrative structure, which was \nconsidered innovative and influenced filmmakers across the world. \n3. Visual techniques: The film is known for its creative use of camera movement, lighting, \ncomposition and editing, particularly in its forest sequences. \n\n \n4. International recognition: Rashomon won the Golden Lion at the Venice Film Festival in \n1951, bringing significant international attention to Japanese cinema. \n5. Global influence: The film influenced filmmakers around the world and popularised the \nstorytelling technique later known as the “Rashomon effect”, where different people \nprovide conflicting accounts of the same event. \nRashomon became a landmark of Japanese cinema through its innovative narrative, visual style \nand exploration of truth and perception, helping establish Japanese cinema on the international \nstage."
